const COVERAGE_EXAM_QUESTIONS = [
{"id":"xb-01","domain":"general","sub":"Cash flow management","q":"Dana and Luis earn $9,000 gross per month. Their housing payment, including taxes and insurance, is $2,000. Other monthly debt payments are $400. Which statement correctly identifies their front-end and back-end ratios?","choices":["Front-end 22%, back-end 27%","Front-end 22%, back-end 33%","Front-end 27%, back-end 33%","Front-end 27%, back-end 22%"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — front-end is $2,000 / $9,000 = 22%; back-end is $2,400 / $9,000 = 27%.","Wrong — 33% would require total debt of $3,000, which the scenario does not include; the back-end ratio is 27%.","Wrong — 27% is the back-end figure, not the front-end; housing alone is $2,000 / $9,000, about 22%.","Wrong — this swaps the two ratios. Front-end is housing only ($2,000 / $9,000); back-end adds all other debt."],"explanation":"Front-end ratio = housing costs / gross income. Back-end ratio = housing plus all other recurring debt / gross income."},
{"id":"xb-02","domain":"general","sub":"Cash flow management","q":"Priya has $18,000 in checking and $22,000 in a money market fund. Her essential monthly expenses are $6,500. Which best describes her liquidity ratio, and what does it tell you?","choices":["About 3.4 months of expenses, counting only the money market fund","About 6.2 months of expenses; she is within the usual 3 to 6 month range","About 9.4 months of expenses; she holds more cash than necessary","About 12.3 months of expenses; she is overly liquid"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — this counts only the money market fund ($22,000 / $6,500, about 3.4 months) and leaves out the checking account.","Correct — liquid assets of $40,000 divided by $6,500 is about 6.2 months, which sits in the usual 3 to 6 month range.","Wrong — this uses a smaller expense base than the one given; the ratio is measured against the stated $6,500 of essential expenses.","Wrong — this doubles the liquid assets before dividing; each dollar of cash is counted once."],"explanation":"Liquidity ratio = liquid assets / monthly essential expenses. Common benchmark: 3 to 6 months, higher if income is variable."},
{"id":"xb-03","domain":"general","sub":"Time value of money concepts and calculations","q":"A client is comparing two plans that each deposit $5,000 per year for 20 years at the same 6% rate. Plan A makes deposits at the end of each year. Plan B makes deposits at the start of each year. All else equal, what is the result?","choices":["Plan B ends with the higher future value only if the rate is negative","Plan A ends with the higher future value, because deposits earn less time in the account","Plan B ends with the higher future value, because each deposit has one extra year of growth","The two plans end with identical future values, because the total deposits are the same"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the timing advantage exists at any positive rate and does not depend on the rate being negative.","Wrong — earlier deposits earn more time, not less; the start-of-year plan has the advantage.","Correct — each start-of-year deposit compounds for one additional year, so the annuity due produces a higher future value.","Wrong — the total amount deposited is equal, but timing changes the growth, so the future values differ."],"explanation":"An annuity due (beginning-of-period payments) has a future value greater than an ordinary annuity by a factor of (1 + i)."},
{"id":"xb-04","domain":"general","sub":"Economic concepts","q":"The Treasury yield curve inverts, with the 2-year yield above the 10-year yield. A client asks what this has historically signaled. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["It means long-term bonds are guaranteed to outperform short-term bonds over the next year","It shows that the Federal Reserve has already begun cutting rates","It confirms that inflation is currently above the Federal Reserve's target","It is a leading indicator that has often preceded economic slowdowns, though not with precise timing"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — an inverted curve does not guarantee any relative bond performance over a specific period.","Wrong — the curve shape reflects market expectations and does not confirm a policy action has already happened.","Wrong — an inverted curve reflects expectations about future rates and growth, not a measurement of current inflation.","Correct — inversions have frequently preceded recessions, but the lead time varies, so it is a signal rather than a timing tool."],"explanation":"Yield curve inversion is a widely watched leading indicator, but the lag from inversion to recession is variable."},
{"id":"xb-05","domain":"general","sub":"Education savings vehicles","q":"Marcus and Tanya opened a 529 plan for their daughter in 2019. In 2026 they want to roll leftover 529 funds into a Roth IRA in her name. Which statement is correct?","choices":["The rollover is not allowed, because the account has not been open at least 15 years","The rollover is allowed up to $35,000 with no annual limit","The rollover is allowed only if the daughter is over age 18 and has earned income","The rollover is allowed now, because the 529 has been open more than five years"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — the 529 must have been maintained for at least 15 years for the beneficiary, which this account has not yet reached.","Wrong — the $35,000 lifetime cap is real, but it applies only after the 15-year requirement is met, and annual Roth contribution limits also apply.","Wrong — the beneficiary's age and earned income are not the test here; the account-age requirement controls.","Wrong — five years is the threshold for other rules (such as some Roth conversion or 529 contribution timing), not for this rollover."],"explanation":"A 529-to-Roth IRA rollover requires a 15-year-old account, is capped at a $35,000 lifetime amount, and is subject to annual Roth contribution limits."},
{"id":"xb-06","domain":"general","sub":"Education savings vehicles","q":"A grandmother wants to contribute to a Coverdell Education Savings Account for her grandson. Which statement about Coverdell ESAs is correct?","choices":["Contributions are limited to $10,000 per year and are not subject to any income phase-out","Annual contributions are limited to $2,000 per beneficiary, from all contributors combined, and phase out at higher incomes","Annual contributions are limited to $2,000 per contributor, so several relatives can each give $2,000","Contributions are unlimited, but the account must be fully distributed by the beneficiary's 18th birthday"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the $10,000 figure is not the Coverdell limit, and phase-outs do apply.","Correct — the $2,000 limit is per beneficiary across all contributors, and contributor income can phase out eligibility.","Wrong — the limit applies per beneficiary, not per contributor, so multiple relatives share one $2,000 cap.","Wrong — contributions are capped, and the funds need not be fully distributed by age 18; the account can run longer."],"explanation":"Coverdell ESAs: $2,000 per beneficiary per year from all sources, income phase-outs for contributors, and qualified K-12 as well as higher-education uses."},
{"id":"xb-07","domain":"general","sub":"Education savings vehicles","q":"A grandfather wants to give $80,000 to his granddaughter for future college costs. He wants to keep control of the money until she is 30. Which vehicle best fits his goal?","choices":["A UTMA custodial account, because the custodian can hold assets until the beneficiary is 30","A 529 plan with the grandfather as owner, because the owner controls distributions indefinitely","A trust, because it can set the age at which the beneficiary gets control beyond the age set by a custodial account","A Coverdell ESA, because it lets the donor retain control until age 30"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — a UTMA must transfer control to the child at the age set by state law, typically 18 or 21, so it cannot delay control to 30.","Wrong — a 529 owner keeps control, but the owner cannot hand the beneficiary control at 30 as a designed feature; the planning need is a trust-level control age.","Correct — a trust lets the grantor specify a distribution or control age beyond the custodial age limit, a key reason to choose a trust.","Wrong — a Coverdell is capped at $2,000 a year, so it cannot hold $80,000 for this purpose."],"explanation":"UTMA/UGMA transfer assets outright at the state termination age. Trusts allow the grantor to set later control dates."},
{"id":"xb-08","domain":"general","sub":"Time value of money concepts and calculations","q":"A client's investments earned 8% over the past year while inflation was 3%. Which figure is the closest approximation of the real return?","choices":["11.0%, found by adding inflation to the nominal return","About 2.4%, found by dividing the inflation rate by the nominal rate","5.0%, found by dividing 8% by 1.6","About 4.9%, found by dividing (1.08 / 1.03) and subtracting 1"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — real return subtracts inflation from the nominal return; adding it increases the figure in the wrong direction.","Wrong — the formula does not divide inflation by the nominal return; that mixes up the inputs.","Wrong — 8% divided by 1.6 has no basis in the real-return formula; the inflation adjustment is a division of growth factors.","Correct — the precise real return is (1.08 / 1.03) minus 1, about 4.85%, which rounds to about 4.9%."],"explanation":"Real return = (1 + nominal) / (1 + inflation) - 1. Simple subtraction is an approximation."},
{"id":"xb-09","domain":"general","sub":"Time value of money concepts and calculations","q":"A client spends $90,000 per year in today's dollars. Assuming 3% annual inflation, which is the closest estimate of the nominal spending needed in 10 years?","choices":["About $121,000","About $135,000","$90,000, because inflation adjusts only investment returns","$99,000"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — $90,000 × 1.03^10 is about $120,950, which rounds to about $121,000.","Wrong — this implies compounding near 4.1% a year, which the stated 3% inflation does not support.","Wrong — inflation erodes purchasing power and raises nominal spending needs; it does not leave them unchanged.","Wrong — this adds about 1% a year in simple terms and ignores compounding."],"explanation":"Future nominal cost = today's cost × (1 + inflation)^years."},
{"id":"xb-10","domain":"general","sub":"Economic concepts","q":"A client asks which inflation measure the Federal Reserve uses for its 2% long-run objective. Which answer is correct?","choices":["The Producer Price Index for finished goods","The personal consumption expenditures (PCE) price index","The GDP deflator for the most recent quarter","The Consumer Price Index for All Urban Consumers (CPI-U)"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the PPI tracks producer-level prices and is a different measure from the Fed's objective.","Correct — the Fed's 2% objective is defined on the PCE price index.","Wrong — the GDP deflator is a broad economic price measure, not the one named in the Fed's objective.","Wrong — CPI is widely reported and used for cost-of-living adjustments, but it is not the Fed's stated target measure."],"explanation":"The FOMC sets its 2% inflation objective in terms of the PCE price index. CPI is used more for cost-of-living and indexing."},
{"id":"xb-11","domain":"general","sub":"Economic concepts","q":"Two assets have the same expected return and the same standard deviation. Their returns have a correlation of 0.2. A client holds 50% of each. If the correlation falls to -0.3 while everything else stays the same, what happens to the portfolio's standard deviation?","choices":["It rises, because negative correlation increases total volatility","It stays the same, because standard deviation depends only on the individual assets","It falls, because lower correlation increases the diversification benefit","It falls to zero, because a negatively correlated pair eliminates all risk"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — lower correlation reduces the combined volatility; it does not raise it.","Wrong — portfolio standard deviation depends on correlation as well as on the individual assets' volatility.","Correct — a lower correlation between holdings produces a larger diversification effect, reducing portfolio standard deviation.","Wrong — perfect negative correlation is the only case that can remove all risk, and -0.3 is not that."],"explanation":"Portfolio variance depends on the weights, the individual variances, and the covariance (correlation times the two standard deviations)."},
{"id":"xb-12","domain":"general","sub":"Financial statements","q":"A client asks which statement shows her net worth on a specific date, and which shows her savings over a year. Which pairing is correct?","choices":["Net worth is not shown on any personal financial statement","Income statement for net worth; balance sheet for savings","Cash flow statement for net worth; balance sheet for savings","Balance sheet for net worth; cash flow statement for savings"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — a personal balance sheet shows net worth directly as assets minus liabilities.","Wrong — the income statement shows income and expenses over a period, not net worth at a point in time.","Wrong — the cash flow statement tracks inflows and outflows over a period, so it does not show net worth at a point in time.","Correct — the balance sheet is a snapshot of assets, liabilities, and net worth on a date; the cash flow statement shows savings over a period."],"explanation":"Balance sheet = point-in-time snapshot (net worth). Cash flow statement = period activity (savings)."},
{"id":"xb-13","domain":"general","sub":"Cash flow management","q":"Kenji earns $150,000 per year. He pays $42,000 in income and payroll taxes and spends $95,000. Which statement correctly describes his savings rate?","choices":["About 12% of after-tax income","About 9% of gross income","About 88% of after-tax income","About 37% of gross income"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — after-tax income is $108,000, and savings of $13,000 is about 12% of it.","Wrong — the savings rate is conventionally measured against after-tax income, not gross income.","Wrong — this is his spending as a share of after-tax income, the complement of his savings rate.","Wrong — this does not match his savings or either base."],"explanation":"Savings rate is usually measured against after-tax income. Savings = after-tax income minus spending."},
{"id":"xb-14","domain":"general","sub":"Financial planning process","q":"A client has three goals: retirement in 25 years, a home down payment in 3 years, and college for a child in 8 years. Her current resources cover only part of all three. What is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Fund the retirement goal fully and leave the others unfunded, since it has the longest horizon","Confirm with the client how she ranks the goals and align the funding with each goal's time horizon and importance to her","Recommend that she postpone all goals until her income rises","Fund all three goals equally so no goal is neglected"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the longest horizon does not automatically make a goal the highest priority; the client's ranking matters.","Correct — the advisor should clarify priorities with the client and match funding and risk to each goal's timing.","Wrong — postponing all goals is not a recommendation that follows from the analysis, and it ignores the goals' stated timelines.","Wrong — equal funding ignores the different time horizons and the client's own priorities."],"explanation":"Goal prioritization depends on the client's values, time horizon, and funding gap. The planner works from the client's ranking."},
{"id":"xb-15","domain":"general","sub":"Gift / income tax strategies","q":"A client in the 32% federal bracket is considering a municipal bond paying 3.5% that is exempt from federal income tax. Which is the closest taxable-equivalent yield?","choices":["About 2.4%","About 3.8%","About 5.1%","About 6.9%"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — this multiplies the tax-free yield by the tax rate, which reverses the calculation.","Wrong — this adds the tax rate as a percentage point to the yield; the adjustment is a division by one minus the tax rate.","Correct — 3.5% / (1 - 0.32) is about 5.15%, the taxable yield that would match the muni's after-tax return.","Wrong — this divides by a smaller factor than the correct one, overstating the equivalent yield."],"explanation":"Taxable-equivalent yield = tax-free yield / (1 - marginal tax rate). It ignores state tax unless the bond is also state-exempt."},
{"id":"xc-01","domain":"risk","sub":"Insurance needs analysis","q":"Ruth, 44, asks how much life insurance she needs. Her debts total $300,000. Income replacement for her family is estimated at $900,000. Education funding needs are $200,000 and final expenses $30,000. She has $500,000 of existing life insurance and $150,000 of liquid savings. Using a needs-based approach, which amount best represents her remaining need?","choices":["$1,430,000, because a needs analysis does not subtract existing resources","$650,000, because liquid savings are subtracted twice","$930,000, because existing life insurance is subtracted and liquid savings are ignored","$780,000, because existing insurance and liquid savings both offset the total need"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — a needs analysis always offsets existing resources; leaving them out overstates the need.","Wrong — liquid savings are counted once, and this result double-subtracts them.","Wrong — this subtracts only the life insurance; the $150,000 of liquid savings also reduces the need.","Correct — total needs of $1,430,000 less $500,000 of insurance and $150,000 of savings leaves $780,000."],"explanation":"Needs approach: add all capital needs, then subtract existing life insurance and liquid assets earmarked for those needs."},
{"id":"xc-02","domain":"risk","sub":"Life insurance (individual and group)","q":"A policyowner misses a premium. The policy is in its 31-day grace period. The insured dies during the grace period. Which statement is correct?","choices":["The death benefit is payable, reduced by the overdue premium","The beneficiary must pay the missed premium before any benefit is paid","The policy is void, and no death benefit is payable","The full death benefit is payable, with no reduction"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — during the grace period coverage continues, and the unpaid premium is subtracted from the death benefit.","Wrong — the beneficiary is not required to pay the premium as a condition of the claim.","Wrong — coverage remains in force during the grace period, so the claim is not void for nonpayment.","Wrong — the overdue premium is deducted from the proceeds in the usual case."],"explanation":"A grace period keeps coverage in force; the death benefit is reduced by any premium due."},
{"id":"xc-03","domain":"risk","sub":"Life insurance (individual and group)","q":"A life insurance policy was issued 18 months ago. The insured dies by suicide. The policy has a standard two-year suicide provision. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["No benefit is payable, because suicide is always excluded for life","The insurer generally pays only the premiums paid, since the claim falls within the suicide provision period","The benefit is payable in full, because the policy was in force for more than one year","The full face amount is payable, because the exclusion applies only to accidental deaths"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the standard suicide provision is time-limited (commonly two years), so it does not exclude suicide for life.","Correct — within the suicide provision period, most policies limit the payout to premiums paid.","Wrong — one year is not the standard threshold; the claim falls inside the typical two-year period.","Wrong — the suicide provision applies to suicide, not to accidents; accidental death is not the issue here."],"explanation":"Standard suicide provisions exclude or limit benefits for a set early period, commonly two years, and then the full benefit applies."},
{"id":"xc-04","domain":"risk","sub":"Life insurance (individual and group)","q":"A policy was issued four years ago. The insurer learns the insured understated her smoking history on the application. She dies today from an unrelated cause. The policy is past its two-year contestable period. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The insurer may void the policy and refund only the premiums paid","The insurer may deny the claim, because misrepresentation can be raised at any time","The insurer generally cannot deny the claim for the misstatement, though it may adjust the benefit to what the premiums would have bought at the correct rate","The insurer must pay the claim in full, and the misstatement can never affect the benefit"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — rescission is generally limited to the contestable period, so a refund of premiums is not the typical remedy here.","Wrong — after the contestability period, most states limit the insurer's ability to deny for misstatements, except for fraud.","Correct — after the contestability period, the usual remedy for a misstatement of health is an adjustment to the benefit, not rescission.","Wrong — a misstatement can still affect the amount payable, and fraud can change the outcome."],"explanation":"Incontestability generally bars rescission after two years; misstatements of age or sex usually adjust the benefit, and fraud is an exception in most states."},
{"id":"xc-05","domain":"risk","sub":"Disability income insurance (individual and group)","q":"A surgeon is disabled and can no longer operate, but she can teach and consult. Her policy is an own-occupation policy with a residual benefit rider. Which best describes the likely outcome?","choices":["The policy pays only after she has been out of work for ten years","No benefit is payable, because she can still work in some capacity","The full benefit is payable, because own-occupation coverage pays the full benefit in every case","A proportional residual benefit is payable, based on the income loss, under the rider's terms"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — no standard policy requires a ten-year wait for a residual benefit.","Wrong — the residual rider exists specifically to pay partial benefits when a person can still work at reduced earnings.","Wrong — own-occupation pays the full benefit only when the insured cannot perform her own occupation; here she can still partly work, so the residual rider matters.","Correct — the residual benefit pays a proportion of the full benefit that matches the percentage of income lost, if the rider's trigger is met."],"explanation":"Residual riders pay a proportional benefit when earnings fall below a defined threshold because of disability."},
{"id":"xc-06","domain":"risk","sub":"Disability income insurance (individual and group)","q":"A dental practice owner wants insurance to keep the office running during a disability, including rent, payroll, and equipment leases, but not his personal living costs. Which coverage best fits?","choices":["Business overhead expense insurance","Key person life insurance on the owner","Long-term care insurance for the owner","Individual disability income insurance on the owner"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — business overhead expense insurance reimburses ordinary fixed business expenses while the owner is disabled.","Wrong — key person life pays on death, not on disability, so it does not cover the practice's expenses during a disability.","Wrong — long-term care pays for care services and does not fund business operating costs.","Wrong — individual disability income replaces the owner's personal income, not the practice's fixed costs."],"explanation":"Business overhead expense (BOE) pays the business's fixed operating costs during disability. Personal disability income covers the owner's own paycheck."},
{"id":"xc-07","domain":"risk","sub":"Principles of risk and insurance","q":"A homeowner's roof is destroyed by hail. Her HO-3 policy is written on a replacement cost basis. Which statement about payment is most accurate?","choices":["Replacement cost policies pay the full replacement cost immediately, with no holdback","Replacement cost policies typically pay actual cash value first and the balance of the replacement cost after repair or replacement is completed","Replacement cost policies pay only the market value of the home","Replacement cost policies never pay for hail damage"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — most replacement cost policies hold back the depreciation until repairs are finished.","Correct — the insurer typically pays actual cash value first, then the recoverable depreciation once the work is complete.","Wrong — replacement cost is based on rebuilding or replacing the damaged property, not on market value.","Wrong — hail is a covered peril under a standard HO-3 for the dwelling, subject to the policy's terms."],"explanation":"Replacement cost policies pay ACV up front and the depreciation holdback after the repair is completed, subject to policy terms."},
{"id":"xc-08","domain":"risk","sub":"Principles of risk and insurance","q":"A client has auto liability limits of $250,000 per person and $500,000 per accident, and a homeowners liability limit of $300,000. He buys a $1,000,000 umbrella policy. In a serious at-fault accident, the total judgment is $1,200,000 and the underlying limits are fully used. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The umbrella pays only if the underlying policies were canceled","The umbrella pays the entire $1,200,000 and the underlying policies pay nothing","The umbrella pays the excess above the underlying limits, subject to its own terms and exclusions","The umbrella replaces the underlying policies, so the client pays nothing at all"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the umbrella is designed to apply on top of active underlying policies, not after they are canceled.","Wrong — the underlying auto policy pays up to its own limit first; the umbrella does not replace it.","Correct — the umbrella responds above the required underlying limits, subject to its own exclusions and conditions.","Wrong — an umbrella supplements underlying coverage and does not eliminate the need for it."],"explanation":"Umbrella coverage is excess liability: it pays after the required underlying limits are exhausted, under its own terms."},
{"id":"xc-09","domain":"risk","sub":"Health insurance and health care cost management (individual and group)","q":"Ben, 66, has been contributing to an HSA through his employer's high-deductible plan. He enrolls in Medicare Part A and Part B this month. Which statement is correct?","choices":["He may contribute up to the family limit because he is now a Medicare beneficiary","He may keep contributing to the HSA as long as he remains in the high-deductible plan","He may contribute only the employer's share, and his own contributions must stop","He can no longer make HSA contributions once he is enrolled in any part of Medicare"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — Medicare enrollment does not increase the contribution limit; it ends eligibility.","Wrong — enrollment in any part of Medicare ends HSA eligibility, even while still in the high-deductible plan.","Wrong — the rule is not split between employer and employee contributions; enrollment in Medicare ends HSA eligibility.","Correct — enrollment in any part of Medicare makes a person ineligible to contribute to an HSA going forward."],"explanation":"HSA eligibility requires HDHP coverage and no enrollment in Medicare. Contributions stop once Medicare begins, though existing funds remain usable."},
{"id":"xc-10","domain":"risk","sub":"Health insurance and health care cost management (individual and group)","q":"A client was laid off and lost employer group health coverage. She elects COBRA continuation. Under the standard rule for termination of employment, how long can COBRA coverage generally continue?","choices":["18 months","24 months","36 months","12 months"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — the standard maximum COBRA period after termination of employment is 18 months.","Wrong — 24 months is not the general standard for job loss; it is not the maximum here.","Wrong — 36 months applies to qualifying events such as divorce or the death of the covered employee, not a layoff.","Wrong — 12 months is shorter than the standard maximum for termination of employment."],"explanation":"COBRA: 18 months for termination or reduced hours; 36 months for many other qualifying events, such as divorce or death of the covered employee."},
{"id":"xc-11","domain":"risk","sub":"Long-term care insurance and long-term care planning (individual and group)","q":"A client wants a long-term care policy that pays when she needs help with daily living. Under the federal tax definition of a qualified long-term care contract, which standard is used to trigger benefits?","choices":["A finding that she requires skilled nursing care at a facility","Inability to perform at least two of six activities of daily living for at least 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment","Any hospitalization lasting longer than 30 days","A physician's statement that she will need care for more than one year"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the trigger is not limited to skilled facility care; home and community care also qualify.","Correct — the federal standard uses a two-of-six ADL test (for at least 90 days) or severe cognitive impairment to trigger benefits.","Wrong — hospitalization length is not the federal benefit trigger for long-term care.","Wrong — a physician's prognosis is not the federal test; the test is based on functional ability or cognition."],"explanation":"Qualified LTC contracts are triggered by inability to perform at least two of six ADLs for 90 days, or severe cognitive impairment."},
{"id":"xc-12","domain":"risk","sub":"Long-term care insurance and long-term care planning (individual and group)","q":"A 58-year-old client wants to protect her assets from Medicaid spend-down if she later needs long-term care. She is considering a partnership-qualified long-term care policy. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Partnership policies pay benefits only in a nursing facility, never at home","Partnership policies require the insured to be on Medicaid before benefits start","Partnership policies let the insured keep assets in an amount equal to benefits paid, on a dollar-for-dollar basis, when she later applies for Medicaid in a participating state","Partnership policies eliminate the Medicaid look-back period entirely"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — partnership policies can cover home and community care as well as facility care.","Wrong — partnership benefits are paid by the insurer, not by Medicaid, and the insured does not need to be on Medicaid first.","Correct — in participating states, assets are protected dollar-for-dollar relative to benefits paid under a qualifying partnership policy.","Wrong — the Medicaid look-back still applies; the partnership benefit works through asset protection, not by removing the look-back."],"explanation":"Partnership programs protect assets dollar-for-dollar against benefits paid, in states that participate. The Medicaid look-back still applies."},
{"id":"xc-13","domain":"risk","sub":"Life insurance (individual and group)","q":"An employer provides group term life insurance of $300,000 to an employee. Under the tax rules for group-term life, which statement is correct?","choices":["Coverage up to $300,000 is always tax-free to the employee","The employee must pay tax only when the policy pays a death benefit","The entire premium is always taxable to the employee as wages","The cost of employer-paid group term coverage above $50,000 is imputed income to the employee, based on an age-based cost table"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — coverage above $50,000 creates imputed income each year, so it is not fully tax-free.","Wrong — the tax on imputed income is recognized each year the coverage is provided, not at death.","Wrong — the first $50,000 of group-term coverage is excluded from income; the taxable amount is the excess only.","Correct — the cost of coverage above $50,000 is imputed as income, measured using the IRS age-based cost table."],"explanation":"Section 79: the first $50,000 of group-term coverage is excluded; the cost of coverage above that is imputed income each year."},
{"id":"xc-14","domain":"risk","sub":"Business owner insurance solutions","q":"Three partners own equal shares of a business and want a cross-purchase buy-sell agreement funded with life insurance. How many policies are needed in total so that each partner is insured for the other two?","choices":["6","9","2","3"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — with a cross-purchase plan, each of the three owners holds a policy on each of the other two, or three times two, which is six policies.","Wrong — nine would require an extra set of policies that the cross-purchase design does not need.","Wrong — two policies would leave one partner's interest unfunded in a three-owner structure.","Wrong — three policies would insure each partner only once, not each partner for the other two."],"explanation":"Cross-purchase plans need n × (n - 1) policies. For three owners, that is 3 × 2 = 6."},
{"id":"xc-15","domain":"risk","sub":"Principles of risk and insurance","q":"A client's homeowners policy excludes flood damage. Her home is in a designated flood zone. Which action best addresses this gap?","choices":["Rely on the umbrella liability policy to cover flood losses","Add a separate flood insurance policy, such as one from the National Flood Insurance Program or a private carrier","Assume the mortgage lender will cover the loss","Increase her personal property limit in the homeowners policy"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — an umbrella policy does not insure her own property against flood; it responds to liability claims.","Correct — flood is typically excluded from homeowners policies and requires separate coverage such as NFIP or private flood insurance.","Wrong — a lender does not pay the homeowner's losses; the lender's interest is protected by a required insurance requirement.","Wrong — raising the personal property limit does not add flood coverage, which remains excluded."],"explanation":"Flood and earthquake are standard exclusions in homeowners policies. Separate coverage is needed, and lenders often require it in designated flood zones."},
{"id":"xc-16","domain":"risk","sub":"Life insurance (individual and group)","q":"A wealthy client wants to keep the death benefit of a $5 million life policy out of his taxable estate. He plans to have an irrevocable life insurance trust own the policy. Which timing rule is most important?","choices":["The trust must be established only after the client reaches age 70","The trust must hold the policy for exactly one year before any premium is paid","If he transfers an existing policy to the trust and dies within three years, the proceeds can still be included in his estate","The policy must be owned by the client for at least ten years before the trust can acquire it"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — there is no age-70 requirement for an ILIT to hold life insurance.","Wrong — the one-year rule does not govern ILIT premium payments or ownership timing.","Correct — a transfer of an existing life policy within three years of death can pull the proceeds back into the estate, so the three-year rule drives the planning.","Wrong — the rule is about a three-year window from transfer to death, not a ten-year ownership period."],"explanation":"Under IRC §2035, proceeds of an existing policy transferred within three years of death can be pulled back into the estate. Having the ILIT apply for and own a new policy from the start avoids that transfer issue."},
{"id":"xe-01","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"On March 1, a client sells 500 shares of XYZ at a $10,000 loss. On March 20, she buys 500 shares of XYZ again because she still wants the position. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The loss is deductible only if she waits 60 days before repurchasing","The loss is treated as a capital gain because the repurchase increases her holdings","The $10,000 loss is fully deductible this year because the sale and purchase happened in the same tax year","The loss is disallowed under the wash-sale rule and is added to the basis of the repurchased shares"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — the rule uses a window of 30 days on each side of the sale, so waiting until day 31 would be needed, not 60 days.","Wrong — a repurchase does not convert a loss into a gain; the loss is deferred through basis adjustment.","Wrong — the wash-sale rule applies within 30 days before or after a sale, regardless of tax year boundaries, so the loss is not deductible.","Correct — repurchasing substantially identical stock within the 61-day window disallows the loss and adds it to the basis of the new shares."],"explanation":"Wash-sale: a loss is disallowed if substantially identical securities are bought within 30 days before or after the sale. The disallowed loss adjusts the basis of the replacement shares."},
{"id":"xe-02","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client has $12,000 of net capital losses for the year and $0 in capital gains. Her taxable income is $80,000 from wages. Which statement about how the loss is used is correct?","choices":["Up to $3,000 offsets ordinary income this year, and the remainder carries forward to future years","Nothing can be used, because capital losses only offset capital gains","The loss expires at the end of the year if unused","The full $12,000 offsets her wages this year"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — up to $3,000 of net capital loss offsets ordinary income each year, and the rest carries forward indefinitely.","Wrong — capital losses can offset up to a set amount of ordinary income, so they are not limited to capital gains.","Wrong — unused capital losses do not expire; they carry forward until used.","Wrong — net capital losses offset ordinary income only up to an annual limit, not without limit."],"explanation":"Net capital loss deduction against ordinary income is limited to $3,000 per year ($1,500 for married filing separately), with an indefinite carryforward."},
{"id":"xe-03","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client bought stock on January 15, 2025, and sold it on January 20, 2026. How should the gain be characterized?","choices":["Short-term, because it was sold in the same calendar year as the new year began","Long-term, because the holding period exceeded one year","Short-term, because the holding period must be measured by calendar years, not days","Long-term, but only if the stock paid a qualified dividend"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the holding period is measured from purchase to sale, not by calendar year.","Correct — the holding period runs from the day after purchase, and more than one year qualifies the gain as long-term.","Wrong — the holding period is measured in days and months from purchase, not in calendar years.","Wrong — dividends do not determine the holding period of the shares; the gain's character depends on the holding period."],"explanation":"Long-term capital gain: asset held more than one year. The holding period starts the day after the acquisition date."},
{"id":"xe-04","domain":"tax","sub":"Income tax fundamentals and calculations","q":"A married couple files jointly with modified adjusted gross income of $300,000. Their net investment income is $40,000. What is their net investment income tax under the 3.8% rule?","choices":["$0","$1,900","$1,520","$11,400"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the couple's MAGI exceeds the joint threshold, so the tax applies.","Wrong — $1,900 applies 3.8% to $50,000, which is the excess of MAGI over the threshold, but the tax is limited to the lesser of net investment income or the excess.","Correct — the tax is 3.8% of the lesser of net investment income ($40,000) or MAGI above $250,000 ($50,000), which is $1,520.","Wrong — $11,400 applies 3.8% to total MAGI, but the tax base is only net investment income or the excess above the threshold."],"explanation":"NIIT = 3.8% × the lesser of (a) net investment income or (b) MAGI above the threshold ($200,000 single, $250,000 MFJ; these thresholds are not indexed)."},
{"id":"xe-05","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A single taxpayer lived in her home for 23 of the last 60 months before selling it at a gain. Which statement about the Section 121 exclusion is correct?","choices":["She qualifies for a partial exclusion only if she was employed in another city","She qualifies only if she rented the home out for the other months","She qualifies for the full exclusion, because the test is about the total time she owned the home","She does not qualify for the exclusion, because the ownership-and-use test requires 24 months within the 5 years before the sale"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — employment-related partial exclusions exist under specific rules, but they are not what this scenario turns on, and she has not met the basic test.","Wrong — renting out the home does not satisfy the use test for the exclusion.","Wrong — the test looks at ownership and use within the five years before the sale, not total ownership time.","Correct — the standard two-of-five test requires at least 24 months of ownership and use within the five years before the sale; she falls short."],"explanation":"Section 121: $250,000 single or $500,000 MFJ exclusion, with at least 2 of the last 5 years of ownership and use. Partial exclusions exist for certain unforeseen circumstances."},
{"id":"xe-06","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax implications of special circumstances","q":"A 16-year-old has $5,000 of unearned income from investments and no earned income. Under the kiddie tax rules, which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The unearned income above the annual threshold is taxed at the parents' marginal rate","All of the income is taxed at the parents' marginal rate, regardless of amount","The income is tax-free because the child is a minor","All of the income is taxed at the child's rate, which is typically zero"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — under the kiddie tax, unearned income above the annual threshold is taxed at the parents' marginal rate.","Wrong — only the amount above the threshold uses the parents' rate, not the entire amount.","Wrong — a child's unearned income is not exempt because the child is a minor.","Wrong — the first amount is taxed at the child's rate, but unearned income above the threshold is taxed at the parents' rate, so not all of it is at zero."],"explanation":"Kiddie tax applies to children under 19 (or under 24 full-time students) with unearned income above a threshold, which is taxed at the parents' marginal rate."},
{"id":"xe-07","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client inherits stock from her father. His basis was $20,000, and the stock was worth $90,000 on the date of death. She sells the stock immediately for $90,000. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["She recognizes a $70,000 short-term capital gain","She recognizes no gain or loss, because her basis is stepped up to the date-of-death value","She recognizes a $20,000 gain, the father's original basis","She recognizes a $70,000 long-term capital gain"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the holding period of inherited property is treated as long-term, and the gain would be zero in any case.","Correct — inherited property generally takes a basis equal to its fair market value at death, so a sale at that value produces no gain.","Wrong — the father's original basis is replaced by the stepped-up basis for inherited assets.","Wrong — the basis is adjusted to the date-of-death value, not kept at the father's original cost."],"explanation":"Under IRC §1014, inherited property takes a basis equal to fair market value at death. Traditional IRA balances do not receive this step-up."},
{"id":"xe-08","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax reduction/management techniques","q":"A client has a traditional IRA with a total balance of $100,000, of which $20,000 is after-tax basis from nondeductible contributions. She converts $20,000 to a Roth IRA this year and has no other IRA balances. How much of the conversion is taxable?","choices":["$0","$4,000","$16,000","$20,000"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the basis is only a fraction of the total IRA balance, so most of the conversion is taxable.","Wrong — $4,000 is the nontaxable portion. The taxable share is the larger 80%, or $16,000.","Correct — under the pro-rata rule, 20% of the conversion is nontaxable ($4,000) and 80% is taxable ($16,000).","Wrong — the nontaxable after-tax portion is excluded from income, so not all of the conversion is taxable."],"explanation":"Pro-rata rule: all traditional IRAs are aggregated. The nontaxable share equals after-tax basis divided by total IRA value, applied to the amount converted."},
{"id":"xe-09","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client sells a property with an adjusted basis of $200,000 for $500,000. She receives $125,000 in the first year and a note for the rest, with payments in later years. Which statement about the first-year gain is correct?","choices":["$125,000, because the entire down payment is recognized as gain","$300,000, because the full profit is recognized at closing regardless of payments","$60,000, because the profit percentage is applied to the note balance","$75,000, found by applying the gross profit percentage to the year-one payment"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — the down payment is not all gain; only the gross profit percentage of it is recognized.","Wrong — under the installment method, gain is recognized as payments are received, not all at closing.","Wrong — the profit percentage applies to cash received, not to the note balance.","Correct — the gross profit percentage is $300,000 / $500,000 = 60%, and 60% of the $125,000 received is $75,000."],"explanation":"Installment method: gain each year = payments received in the year × gross profit percentage. Depreciation recapture is recognized in the year of sale regardless of payments."},
{"id":"xe-10","domain":"tax","sub":"Income tax fundamentals and calculations","q":"A client's modified adjusted gross income is $130,000. He actively participates in a rental property that produces a $30,000 loss and has no other passive income. Under the special allowance rules, how much of the loss can he deduct this year?","choices":["$10,000, because the $25,000 allowance is reduced by 50% of MAGI above $100,000","$0, because rental losses are never deductible against wages","$30,000, because all rental losses are deductible for active participants","$25,000, because the full special allowance applies at any income level"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — the allowance is $25,000 minus 50% of MAGI above $100,000 = $25,000 - $15,000 = $10,000, and the other $20,000 is suspended.","Wrong — active participants can use a limited allowance against nonpassive income.","Wrong — the active-participation allowance is capped at $25,000 and is reduced at higher income levels.","Wrong — the allowance phases out above $100,000 of MAGI, so the full $25,000 is not available here."],"explanation":"Active-participation rental allowance: $25,000, reduced by 50% of MAGI above $100,000 and fully phased out at $150,000. Disallowed losses carry forward."},
{"id":"xe-11","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client wants to exchange a valuable painting she holds for investment for another painting, deferring the gain under a like-kind exchange. Which statement is correct?","choices":["The exchange qualifies, because both assets are artwork and therefore like-kind","The exchange does not qualify, because like-kind treatment under Section 1031 applies only to real property","The exchange qualifies only if the paintings are held for at least ten years","The exchange qualifies if the client receives cash in the transaction"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — artwork is personal property, and Section 1031 no longer covers personal property after 2017.","Correct — since 2018, like-kind exchange treatment under Section 1031 is limited to real property held for investment or business use.","Wrong — the holding period does not change the outcome; personal property is excluded from Section 1031 regardless.","Wrong — receiving cash does not make a personal-property exchange eligible; it may trigger recognition of gain."],"explanation":"The Tax Cuts and Jobs Act limited Section 1031 to real property. Exchanges of art, equipment, and other personal property no longer qualify."},
{"id":"xe-12","domain":"tax","sub":"Charitable/philanthropic contributions and deductions","q":"A 72-year-old client has not yet reached her required beginning date for RMDs. She wants to make a qualified charitable distribution (QCD) from her IRA. Which statement is correct?","choices":["She can make a QCD, but the amount is fully taxable and does not count toward any RMD","She cannot make a QCD until she reaches the RMD age","She can make a QCD now, because QCDs are available from age 70½, and the amount generally excludes from income","She can make a QCD only if she itemizes deductions"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — a QCD is excluded from income, not taxable, and it counts toward her RMD once that requirement applies.","Wrong — QCDs begin at age 70½, which is earlier than the RMD age, so she qualifies.","Correct — a QCD from an IRA can be made from age 70½, and the distribution is excluded from taxable income up to the annual limit.","Wrong — a QCD is an exclusion from income, so it works whether or not she itemizes."],"explanation":"QCD: from an IRA, age 70½ or older, paid directly to a qualifying charity; excluded from income up to the indexed annual limit, and counts toward any RMD."},
{"id":"xe-13","domain":"tax","sub":"Fundamental and current tax law","q":"A client gives her adult child $50,000 in cash this year. She has made no other taxable gifts. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The full $50,000 is subject to gift tax at a 40% rate","The gift is tax-free and does not need to be reported, because it is cash","No gift tax return is required, because the gift is under $100,000","A gift tax return is required, the first $19,000 is excluded, and the $31,000 excess reduces her lifetime exemption without tax due"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — gift tax applies only to the amount above the lifetime exemption, not to every dollar of the gift at a flat 40%.","Wrong — cash gifts are not exempt, and gifts above the annual exclusion must be reported.","Wrong — the threshold for a gift tax return is any gift above the annual exclusion, not a $100,000 figure.","Correct — the 2026 annual exclusion is $19,000 per recipient, so the $31,000 excess is reported on Form 709 and uses part of the lifetime exemption, with no tax owed."],"explanation":"Gifts above the annual exclusion require Form 709 but generally do not trigger tax until the lifetime exemption is used up."},
{"id":"xe-14","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax consequences of property transactions","q":"A client sells a collectible coin held for three years at a long-term gain. Which statement about the federal rate on that gain is correct?","choices":["The gain is taxed at a maximum rate of 28%, which is higher than the top long-term rate for most other assets","The gain is taxed at 3.8%, the net investment income tax rate, and no income tax applies","The gain is taxed at the 0%, 15%, or 20% long-term rates, the same as stock","The gain is taxed at ordinary income rates, with no preferential treatment"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — long-term gain on collectibles is taxed at a maximum rate of 28%, which can exceed the 20% rate that applies to most long-term gains.","Wrong — the 3.8% NIIT is an additional tax on top of the income tax, not the rate on the gain itself.","Wrong — collectibles do not get the standard long-term rates that apply to most capital assets like stock.","Wrong — collectibles have a specific long-term maximum rate, so they do not simply follow ordinary rates."],"explanation":"Long-term gain on collectibles (art, coins, antiques, precious metals in many cases) is taxed at a maximum 28% rate."},
{"id":"xe-15","domain":"tax","sub":"Tax implications of special circumstances","q":"A retired client receives $30,000 of Social Security benefits and $20,000 of other income, including $10,000 of tax-exempt municipal bond interest. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["Municipal bond interest is excluded when calculating how much of the Social Security benefit is taxable, so it has no effect","Municipal bond interest is added to provisional income, which can cause more of the Social Security benefit to be taxable","Municipal bond interest is taxable, so it is treated the same as ordinary income for all purposes","Social Security benefits are never taxable when the client has tax-exempt income"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — tax-exempt interest is included in provisional income for Social Security taxability, even though it is not taxed directly.","Correct — provisional income includes tax-exempt interest, which can raise the taxable share of Social Security benefits.","Wrong — municipal interest is generally exempt from federal income tax, although it counts in the Social Security calculation.","Wrong — the exemption of other income does not remove Social Security taxation, which depends on provisional income."],"explanation":"Provisional income = AGI (without SS) + tax-exempt interest + 50% of SS benefits. Tax-exempt interest increases the amount of benefits that may be taxable."},
{"id":"xf-01","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"Maria was born in 1965 and her full retirement age is 67. She claims her Social Security retirement benefit at 62. Her primary insurance amount (PIA) is $2,000 per month. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["She receives nothing until full retirement age, so claiming at 62 is not possible","She receives $2,000 per month, because the claiming age does not affect the benefit","She receives a permanently reduced benefit, because claiming before full retirement age reduces benefits, with the reduction depending on how many months early she claims","She receives $2,000 per month until 67, then a higher amount, because early claiming increases the benefit"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — benefits can be claimed as early as age 62, subject to the reduction.","Wrong — the claiming age has a direct effect on the monthly amount, so $2,000 is only the amount at full retirement age.","Correct — claiming before FRA permanently reduces the benefit, based on the months before FRA.","Wrong — early claiming reduces the benefit, and it is not restored at 67."],"explanation":"Claiming at 62 for FRA 67 reduces the benefit to about 70% of PIA. The reduction is permanent (aside from limited situations)."},
{"id":"xf-02","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"A client's full retirement age is 67. He delays claiming until 70 and his PIA is $2,400 per month. Which statement about his benefit is most accurate?","choices":["His benefit decreases after full retirement age, because the credits expire","His benefit at 70 is the same as at 62, because the increase is capped at full retirement age","His benefit is $2,400 per month, because delaying past full retirement age has no effect","His benefit increases by delayed retirement credits for each month after full retirement age, up to age 70, so the benefit at 70 is higher than the PIA"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — delayed retirement credits do not expire; they increase the benefit while he delays.","Wrong — the benefit at 62 is reduced, so it is lower, not the same as at 70.","Wrong — delaying past full retirement age earns delayed retirement credits, which increase the benefit.","Correct — delayed retirement credits accrue for each month between FRA and age 70, so the benefit is higher than the PIA at 70."],"explanation":"Delayed retirement credits increase the benefit for each year claimed after FRA, up to age 70, with no further gain beyond that age."},
{"id":"xf-03","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"A 63-year-old client is still working and has not reached full retirement age. He claims Social Security and earns more than the annual earnings limit for that year. Which statement is correct?","choices":["His benefits are reduced by $1 for every $2 of earnings above the limit","His benefits are not affected, because the earnings test applies only after full retirement age","His benefits are permanently forfeited, regardless of later earnings","His benefits are reduced by $1 for every $1 of earnings above the limit"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — $1 is withheld for every $2 of earnings above the annual limit before full retirement age.","Wrong — the earnings test applies before full retirement age, which is exactly when he is subject to it.","Wrong — withheld amounts are recalculated, and benefits are increased at FRA; they are not forfeited.","Wrong — the earnings test is $1 withheld for every $2 above the limit, not $1 for every $1."],"explanation":"Before FRA, $1 of benefits is withheld for every $2 of earnings above the annual limit. In the year of FRA, a higher threshold applies, with a $1-for-$3 reduction."},
{"id":"xf-04","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"A retired couple files jointly and has provisional income of $40,000. Their Social Security benefits total $30,000. Which statement is most accurate about taxation of Social Security?","choices":["None of their benefits are taxable, because they are under $100,000 of income","Up to 50% of their benefits may be taxable, because provisional income is above the joint base amount but below the upper threshold","Up to 85% of their benefits may be taxable, because provisional income is above the upper threshold","All of their benefits are taxable, because they are married filing jointly"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the thresholds are based on provisional income, not total income, and they are $32,000 base and $44,000 upper for joint filers.","Correct — with provisional income between the base and upper thresholds, up to 50% of benefits can be taxable.","Wrong — 85% applies only above the upper threshold, which this couple has not crossed at $40,000.","Wrong — taxation is capped at 85%, and it depends on provisional income."],"explanation":"Provisional income = AGI (excluding SS) + tax-exempt interest + 50% of SS. Joint thresholds are $32,000 and $44,000; single thresholds are $25,000 and $34,000. These thresholds are not indexed."},
{"id":"xf-05","domain":"retirement","sub":"Qualified plan rules and options","q":"A married participant in a defined benefit plan is choosing between a single-life annuity and a joint and survivor annuity. Under federal rules, which statement is correct?","choices":["The spouse automatically receives 100% of the pension after retirement, regardless of election","Only the participant's employer decides which option applies","The spouse must consent in writing if the participant elects an option that does not provide a survivor benefit","The participant may choose any option without spousal involvement"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the survivor benefit is a choice that depends on the option elected, not an automatic 100% benefit.","Wrong — the participant, with spousal consent where required, makes the election, not the employer.","Correct — qualified plans generally require the spouse's written, notarized (or witnessed) consent to waive the qualified joint and survivor annuity.","Wrong — federal rules generally require spousal consent to waive the survivor annuity, so the participant cannot choose freely."],"explanation":"Under ERISA, the qualified joint and survivor annuity is the default for married participants. Waiving it requires notarized or witnessed spousal consent."},
{"id":"xf-06","domain":"retirement","sub":"Distribution rules and taxation","q":"A client born in 1958 turned 73 this year. She is retired and her traditional IRA had a balance of $500,000 on December 31 of last year. Which statement about her first RMD is most accurate?","choices":["Her RMD is based on the current year-end balance, so she cannot calculate it until December 31","Her first RMD is due by December 31 of this year, based on the prior year-end balance and her life expectancy factor","Her first RMD is not due until age 75 under current law","Her first RMD is due by April 1 of next year, computed from the prior year-end balance and the Uniform Lifetime Table; taking it then means two RMDs in that year"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — the calculation uses the prior year-end balance, not the current year-end balance.","Wrong — the first RMD is not due by December 31 of the year she reaches the age; that deadline applies to later years, and the first one may be delayed to April 1.","Wrong — age 75 applies only to people born in 1960 or later; she was born in 1958, so the RMD age is 73.","Correct — the first RMD may be taken by April 1 of the following year, using the prior year-end balance and the Uniform Lifetime Table. Delaying it means two distributions fall in that year."],"explanation":"RMD age is 73 for those born 1951 to 1959 and 75 for those born 1960 or later. The first RMD can be delayed to April 1 of the following year, which means two RMDs in that year."},
{"id":"xf-07","domain":"retirement","sub":"Distribution rules and taxation","q":"A non-spouse adult child inherits a traditional IRA from a parent who died in 2026 after reaching RMD age. The child is not disabled, chronically ill, or a minor. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The child must fully distribute the account by the end of the tenth year after death, and annual distributions are required during that period if the parent had started RMDs","The child is not required to take any distributions, because the account is inherited","The child must distribute the account within five years, regardless of the parent's RMD status","The child can stretch distributions over her own life expectancy"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — under the 10-year rule, the account is depleted by year 10, and annual RMDs apply when the owner had already begun RMDs.","Wrong — the account is subject to distribution rules, and an inherited traditional IRA is not exempt from them.","Wrong — the five-year rule applies in some cases where the owner died before RMDs began, not in this situation.","Wrong — life-expectancy stretch is available only to eligible designated beneficiaries, which this child is not."],"explanation":"Non-eligible designated beneficiaries: 10-year rule. If the owner died after the RMD start, annual distributions must continue in years 1 through 9 as well."},
{"id":"xf-08","domain":"retirement","sub":"Types of retirement plans","q":"A client, age 57, opened a Roth IRA 3 years ago with a $30,000 contribution. He wants to withdraw the full $30,000 now for a home purchase. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["He may withdraw the earnings tax-free but must pay tax on the contributions","He may withdraw the contributions at any time, tax-free and penalty-free, because contributions to a Roth IRA are always accessible","He may not withdraw anything, because the five-year rule blocks all distributions for 5 years","He must pay tax and a 10% penalty on the full amount"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — contributions come out first and are not taxed; earnings are the portion subject to tests.","Correct — Roth IRA contributions can be withdrawn at any time, free of tax and penalty, because they were made with after-tax dollars.","Wrong — the five-year rule applies to earnings and certain conversions, not to the return of contributions.","Wrong — contributions are returned first, so the full amount is not taxable or penalized."],"explanation":"Roth IRA ordering: contributions come out first, tax- and penalty-free. Earnings are subject to the five-year rule and age 59½ tests."},
{"id":"xf-09","domain":"retirement","sub":"Distribution rules and taxation","q":"A client leaves his employer at age 56. He has a 401(k) and wants to access the money without the 10% additional tax. Which statement is correct?","choices":["He must wait until 59½ to avoid the 10% additional tax from any source","The exception applies only if he rolls the 401(k) to an IRA first","The rule of 55 exception allows penalty-free withdrawals from the employer plan, but only if he separated from service in or after the year he turned 55","The exception applies only to IRAs, not 401(k)s"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the rule of 55 is an exception to the 10% penalty and avoids the wait for 59½ in the employer plan.","Wrong — rolling to an IRA removes the exception, because IRA distributions do not use the separation-from-service rule.","Correct — the separation-from-service exception allows penalty-free distributions from the employer plan if he leaves in or after the year he reaches 55.","Wrong — the rule applies to employer plans like 401(k)s; it does not apply to IRAs, which is the reverse of this option."],"explanation":"Rule of 55: if you separate from service in or after the year you turn 55, distributions from that employer's plan are exempt from the 10% penalty. Rolling to an IRA can cause the loss of this exception."},
{"id":"xf-10","domain":"retirement","sub":"Retirement income and distribution strategies","q":"A client plans to retire with a $1,000,000 portfolio. Early in retirement the market falls 25%, and he withdraws the same inflation-adjusted amount each year. Which risk does this best illustrate?","choices":["Inflation risk","Reinvestment risk","Longevity risk","Sequence-of-returns risk"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — inflation risk concerns purchasing power, not the timing of market losses relative to withdrawals.","Wrong — reinvestment risk is about reinvesting cash flows at lower rates, not about early losses with withdrawals.","Wrong — longevity risk is the risk of outliving assets; this scenario is about the timing of losses early in retirement.","Correct — poor returns early in retirement, combined with withdrawals, can deplete the portfolio faster than average returns would suggest."],"explanation":"Sequence-of-returns risk: early losses combined with withdrawals permanently impair the portfolio, even if later returns average out."},
{"id":"xf-11","domain":"retirement","sub":"Retirement income and distribution strategies","q":"A client asks whether the traditional 4% withdrawal guideline is a guarantee. Which response is most accurate?","choices":["No, it is a historical rule of thumb based on a set of past market scenarios, and results depend on market returns, inflation, and spending","Yes, if the portfolio is 60% stocks, the rule is guaranteed","No, the rule applies only to portfolios held entirely in bonds","Yes, the 4% rule guarantees that the portfolio will last 30 years in every market"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — the 4% rule is a rule of thumb derived from historical simulations and is sensitive to returns, inflation, fees, and spending flexibility.","Wrong — allocation does not turn the rule into a guarantee, although it affects outcomes.","Wrong — the rule was developed from balanced stock and bond portfolios, not bond-only ones.","Wrong — the rule is based on historical data and does not guarantee outcomes for every future market path."],"explanation":"The 4% rule is a historical planning heuristic; outcomes depend on sequence of returns, inflation, fees, and whether spending adjusts."},
{"id":"xf-12","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"A client turns 65 this year and plans to delay Medicare Part B enrollment because he is still covered by his employer's group health plan through his own active employment. Which statement is correct?","choices":["He must enroll in Part B at 65 to avoid any penalty, even while still working","He may delay Part B without penalty if he is covered by a group plan from current employment, and he has a special enrollment period when that coverage ends","He cannot delay Part B under any circumstances after age 65","He will automatically be enrolled in Part B at 67 without any action"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — delaying is allowed under this specific situation without a late-enrollment penalty.","Correct — coverage from current employment with an employer of 20 or more employees generally allows a special enrollment period without a late penalty.","Wrong — delaying is allowed in the employment-coverage case, so the rule is not absolute.","Wrong — there is no automatic enrollment at 67; enrollment requires action or qualifying coverage status."],"explanation":"Medicare Part B late penalty: 10% per 12-month period of delay, unless delayed due to qualifying employer group coverage, with an eight-month special enrollment period after that coverage ends."},
{"id":"xf-13","domain":"retirement","sub":"Social Security and Medicare planning","q":"A client's modified adjusted gross income from two years ago was high because of a large Roth conversion. Which statement about Medicare Part B premiums is correct?","choices":["IRMAA is based on the client's net worth, not income","Income-related monthly adjustment amounts (IRMAA) are based on the client's income from the current year only","IRMAA is based on income from two years prior, so a high-income year can raise premiums two years later","IRMAA does not apply to Part B premiums, only to Part D"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — IRMAA is based on income, not on net worth.","Wrong — IRMAA uses income from two years earlier, not the current year.","Correct — IRMAA is calculated on MAGI from the tax return two years before the premium year, so a conversion can raise premiums later.","Wrong — IRMAA applies to both Part B and Part D premiums."],"explanation":"IRMAA uses MAGI from two years prior. Life-changing events such as retirement can be reported to request a reduction."},
{"id":"xf-14","domain":"retirement","sub":"Distribution rules and taxation","q":"A client receives a lump-sum distribution from his employer's 401(k), including company stock with a cost basis of $40,000 and a fair market value of $140,000. He does not roll the stock to an IRA. Which treatment of the net unrealized appreciation (NUA) is correct?","choices":["The NUA is tax-free because it is company stock","The $100,000 NUA is taxed as ordinary income, and the $40,000 basis is taxed as long-term capital gain","The full $140,000 is taxable as ordinary income in the year of distribution","The $40,000 cost basis is taxed as ordinary income, and the $100,000 NUA is taxed at long-term capital gain rates when the shares are sold"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — NUA is not tax-free; it is deferred and taxed as capital gain when the shares are sold.","Wrong — the classification is reversed: basis is ordinary income, and the NUA is capital gain.","Wrong — the NUA portion is not taxed as ordinary income at distribution if the stock is distributed in-kind and the rules are followed.","Correct — the cost basis is ordinary income at distribution, and the NUA is taxed as long-term gain when the stock is later sold, under the NUA rules."],"explanation":"NUA: the employer stock distributed in kind after a lump sum; cost basis taxed as ordinary income at distribution; the NUA is taxed as long-term capital gain on sale, regardless of holding period after distribution."},
{"id":"xf-15","domain":"retirement","sub":"Distribution rules and taxation","q":"A client buys a nonqualified deferred annuity with $200,000 of after-tax premiums. The contract value grows to $260,000. He takes a lump-sum withdrawal of $60,000. Which statement about the tax treatment is most accurate, under the general rule for nonqualified annuities?","choices":["The $60,000 is taxed as ordinary income up to the gain of $60,000, which is the earnings portion withdrawn first","The $60,000 is taxed as long-term capital gain, because annuities are capital assets","Only $20,000 is taxable, because withdrawals are divided by the ratio of basis to total value","The full $60,000 is tax-free because it is return of principal"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — under the last-in, first-out rule for nonqualified deferred annuities, earnings come out first and are taxed as ordinary income, up to the total gain of $60,000.","Wrong — annuity earnings are ordinary income, not long-term capital gain.","Wrong — the pro-rata approach applies to annuitized payments through the exclusion ratio, not to a lump-sum withdrawal under the general rule.","Wrong — under the general rule, withdrawals from a deferred annuity are income first, so the gain comes out before basis."],"explanation":"Nonqualified deferred annuities: LIFO for withdrawals. Earnings come out first and are taxed as ordinary income. Annuitized payments use the exclusion ratio instead."},
{"id":"xg-01","domain":"estate","sub":"Property titling and beneficiary designations","q":"Helen owns a brokerage account payable on death to her son, a house titled in her name alone with no transfer-on-death deed, and a will leaving everything to her daughter. Which asset passes through probate?","choices":["The brokerage account, because a POD designation must be probated","The house, because it is titled in her name alone and no transfer-on-death deed exists","None of the assets, because the will is not effective until probated","The brokerage account, because the will controls every asset"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — a valid POD designation transfers outside probate, so the account does not go through probate.","Correct — sole-name real estate with no transfer-on-death deed generally passes through probate under the will.","Wrong — the will is probated only for assets that are probate assets; the house is one of them, so this is not the right answer.","Wrong — a will controls only probate assets; a POD designation on the account passes outside the will."],"explanation":"Probate assets: sole-name property without beneficiary designations or survivorship. Non-probate: POD/TOD accounts, joint property with survivorship, retirement plans with beneficiaries, and trust-owned assets."},
{"id":"xg-02","domain":"estate","sub":"Property titling and beneficiary designations","q":"A client's will leaves his entire estate to his second wife. His 401(k) names his first wife as beneficiary and has not been updated. He dies. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The 401(k) goes to the client's estate and passes under the will","The second wife receives the 401(k), because the will governs all assets","The first wife receives the 401(k), because the beneficiary designation controls the account regardless of the will","The 401(k) is split equally between the wives under state law"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — the designation keeps the account out of the estate, so it does not pass under the will.","Wrong — a will generally does not override a beneficiary designation on a retirement plan.","Correct — a valid beneficiary designation on a 401(k) governs distribution, regardless of what the will says.","Wrong — the account does not split by state law when a valid designation exists."],"explanation":"Beneficiary designations on retirement accounts and life insurance override the will. Keeping them current matters after a divorce or remarriage."},
{"id":"xg-03","domain":"estate","sub":"Estate and incapacity planning documents","q":"A married man with two children dies without a will. His wife survives him. Under typical intestacy rules in most states, which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The estate goes to the state, because intestacy does not recognize heirs","The wife receives everything, because spouses always inherit the entire estate under intestacy","The children inherit everything, because the spouse has no intestate rights","The estate is shared among the spouse and children under the state's statutory formula, with the spouse receiving a defined share"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — intestate property passes to heirs under statute, not to the state, unless there are no heirs.","Wrong — intestacy does not give a spouse the entire estate in every state; the formula splits the estate with descendants in many cases.","Wrong — a surviving spouse has intestate rights in every state.","Correct — the state's intestacy statute divides the estate among the spouse and descendants under a formula that varies by state."],"explanation":"Intestacy is governed by state statute, and the split between spouse and children differs by state. Without a will, the court applies that formula."},
{"id":"xg-04","domain":"estate","sub":"Types, features, and taxation of trusts","q":"A client wants to avoid probate for his real estate in two states and keep his affairs private, and he still wants to control assets during his lifetime. Which tool best fits?","choices":["A revocable living trust that holds the real estate and other major assets","An irrevocable life insurance trust","A testamentary trust created inside his will","A joint tenancy with his adult son on all accounts"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — a revocable living trust avoids probate in each state where property is titled to the trust, stays private, and lets the grantor manage assets during life.","Wrong — an ILIT holds life insurance and is irrevocable, so it does not give the grantor control of other assets.","Wrong — a testamentary trust is created by the will and is subject to probate, so it does not avoid it.","Wrong — joint tenancy with a son exposes assets to his creditors and can create gift and control issues, so it is not the best fit."],"explanation":"A revocable living trust avoids probate for trust-titled assets (ancillary probate can otherwise occur in other states) and does not reduce estate tax."},
{"id":"xg-05","domain":"estate","sub":"Marital deduction","q":"A husband dies in 2026 and leaves his entire estate to his U.S.-citizen wife. His executor does not file a Form 706 for him. Which statement is correct?","choices":["The wife cannot use any of his unused exclusion amount, because no Form 706 was filed","The wife can use his unused exclusion amount only if the executor timely files Form 706 with the portability election","The unused exclusion amount expires after one year","The wife's estate can still use his unused exclusion amount, because portability is automatic"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the executor can still elect portability by timely filing Form 706, even without a tax due.","Correct — the deceased spouse's unused exclusion amount is available only if the executor timely files Form 706 and makes the portability election.","Wrong — there is no one-year expiration; the question is whether the election was made.","Wrong — portability is not automatic; it requires an affirmative election on a timely filed return."],"explanation":"Portability (DSUE): the surviving spouse can use the deceased spouse's unused exclusion only with a timely filed Form 706 and the election, even if no tax is due."},
{"id":"xg-06","domain":"estate","sub":"Types, features, and taxation of trusts","q":"A husband wants his second wife to receive income from a trust for her life, with the remainder going to his children from his first marriage after her death. Which trust is most appropriate, and why?","choices":["A charitable remainder trust, because it gives the children a tax deduction","A grantor retained annuity trust, because it removes the assets from the wife's estate","A QTIP trust, because it qualifies for the marital deduction at his death while the remainder passes to his children","A spendthrift trust, because it prevents the wife from transferring her interest"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — a charitable remainder trust benefits charity as the remainder, not the children.","Wrong — a GRAT is a lifetime planning tool for a grantor, not a way to provide spousal income at death with a remainder to children.","Correct — a QTIP trust gives the spouse income for life, qualifies for the marital deduction, and passes the remainder to the children as the husband directs; the trust is included in the wife's estate at her death.","Wrong — a spendthrift provision protects against creditors, but it does not provide the marital deduction or control the remainder."],"explanation":"QTIP: income to a surviving spouse for life, a QTIP election for the marital deduction, and the remainder under the grantor's terms. The property is included in the spouse's estate at her death."},
{"id":"xg-07","domain":"estate","sub":"Gift, estate, and GST tax compliance and calculation","q":"A grandmother wants to pay her granddaughter's private college tuition directly to the university, and she also wants to give the granddaughter $19,000 in cash for living expenses this year. Which statement is correct?","choices":["Both amounts are subject to the annual exclusion, so the tuition uses $19,000 of it","Tuition paid directly is taxable, because direct payments count as gifts above the annual exclusion","The grandmother must file Form 709 for the tuition payment, which eliminates the cash gift's exclusion","Tuition paid directly to the school is excluded from gift tax without limit, and the $19,000 cash gift uses the annual exclusion"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — the direct tuition payment is not subject to the annual exclusion; its exclusion is separate and unlimited.","Wrong — direct payments of tuition are excluded from gift tax when paid to the school, so they are not taxable gifts.","Wrong — a direct tuition payment does not require a Form 709 when it meets the education exclusion, and the cash gift is a separate matter.","Correct — direct tuition payments to an educational institution are excluded without limit under the education exclusion, separate from the annual exclusion for the cash gift."],"explanation":"IRC §2503(e): unlimited exclusion for tuition paid directly to a qualifying educational institution. Medical payments directly to providers are excluded in a similar way. The annual exclusion is separate."},
{"id":"xg-08","domain":"estate","sub":"Strategies to transfer property","q":"A client holds stock with a very low basis and wants to sell it without an immediate capital gains tax, while providing income to himself for life and leaving the remainder to charity. Which strategy best fits?","choices":["A charitable remainder trust, which pays the client an income for life and passes the remainder to charity","A grantor retained annuity trust, which pays the client income and transfers the stock to heirs tax-free","A private foundation funded with the stock, which pays the client an income for life","A charitable lead annuity trust, which pays income to charity and the remainder to the client's heirs"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — a CRT lets the trust sell the appreciated stock without immediate capital gain, pays the client an income stream, and passes the remainder to charity.","Wrong — a GRAT is a family transfer tool and does not provide a charitable remainder.","Wrong — a private foundation does not pay the donor an income stream, and it does not allow the donor to receive the income from a sale in the same way.","Wrong — a CLAT reverses the structure: charity gets the income first and the heirs get the remainder."],"explanation":"CRT: the trust sells appreciated assets without immediate tax, pays income to the grantor for life or a term, and the remainder goes to charity. The grantor gets a partial income tax deduction."},
{"id":"xg-09","domain":"estate","sub":"Estate and incapacity planning documents","q":"A client wants a document that lets his daughter manage his finances if he becomes incapacitated, and he wants the authority to end at his death. Which document best fits?","choices":["A living trust with no successor trustee","A durable power of attorney for finances","A last will and testament","A healthcare proxy"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — a trust with no successor trustee does not give anyone authority to act if the client becomes incapacitated.","Correct — a durable power of attorney allows an agent to manage financial matters during incapacity; its authority ends at death, and the will and trust then take over.","Wrong — a will takes effect at death and does not give anyone authority during incapacity.","Wrong — a healthcare proxy covers medical decisions, not financial management."],"explanation":"Durable POA: financial authority during life, surviving incapacity. Its authority ends at death, when the estate plan (will or trust) controls."},
{"id":"xg-10","domain":"estate","sub":"Property titling and beneficiary designations","q":"A married couple owns their home as tenants by the entirety in a state that recognizes this form. Which statement is most accurate?","choices":["The home is split equally between the spouses and each share goes through probate","Either spouse can sell the home alone at any time without the other's consent","The home passes to the surviving spouse outside probate, and generally cannot be reached by one spouse's individual creditors","The home passes under the will of the first spouse to die, and creditors can reach it at any time"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — entireties property is not split and probated; it passes by survivorship.","Wrong — tenancy by the entirety generally requires both spouses to act together, so one cannot sell alone.","Correct — survivorship passes the home outside probate, and individual creditors of one spouse generally cannot reach entireties property.","Wrong — tenancy by the entirety passes by survivorship, not under the will, and it is protected from individual creditors."],"explanation":"Tenancy by the entirety: survivorship and protection from individual creditors in states that recognize it, available only to married couples."},
{"id":"xg-11","domain":"estate","sub":"Types, features, and taxation of trusts","q":"A trustee manages a trust for a surviving spouse (income beneficiary) and the couple's children (remainder beneficiaries). The trustee invests heavily in high-yield bonds that maximize income but lose principal value. Which fiduciary duty is most at issue?","choices":["The duty to avoid conflicts of interest with the trust's corporate affiliates","The duty of confidentiality owed to the children","The duty of loyalty, because the trustee benefits personally from the investments","The duty of impartiality, because the trustee favors the income beneficiary over the remainder beneficiaries"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — no corporate affiliate is involved in the facts.","Wrong — confidentiality is not the issue; the question is the balance of benefits between beneficiaries.","Wrong — nothing in the facts says the trustee benefits personally; the issue is how the portfolio balances the two groups.","Correct — a trustee must act impartially between income and remainder beneficiaries, and shifting value toward income at the expense of principal can violate that duty."],"explanation":"Duty of impartiality: balance the interests of current income and remainder beneficiaries. Under prudent investor rules, the trustee also considers diversification and the trust's purpose."},
{"id":"xg-12","domain":"estate","sub":"Types, features, and taxation of trusts","q":"A client plans to transfer $3 million of appreciating stock to an irrevocable trust for his children, and he wants to keep the right to receive annuity payments for ten years. Which trust best fits this goal of shifting appreciation out of his estate?","choices":["A grantor retained annuity trust (GRAT), which pays an annuity to the grantor for a set term and passes remaining appreciation to the children","A qualified terminable interest property (QTIP) trust, which passes assets to the spouse","A charitable lead annuity trust, which pays annuities to charity and gives the remainder to the grantor","A spendthrift trust, which pays annuities to the beneficiary and protects assets from creditors"],"correct":0,"rationale":["Correct — a GRAT pays the grantor an annuity for a set term, and any appreciation above the IRS hurdle rate passes to the children with little or no gift tax.","Wrong — a QTIP is a marital-deduction trust for a spouse; it does not shift appreciation to children during the grantor's life.","Wrong — a CLAT pays charity first and the remainder to the grantor, which reverses the goal here.","Wrong — a spendthrift provision protects against creditors and does not move appreciation out of the estate."],"explanation":"GRAT: a retained annuity for a term; if the grantor survives the term, appreciation above the IRS hurdle passes to beneficiaries outside the estate. If the grantor dies during the term, the assets are included."},
{"id":"xg-13","domain":"estate","sub":"Gift, estate, and GST tax compliance and calculation","q":"A grandfather wants to give $50,000 directly to his grandson, who is the grandson of the grandfather's child. The grandfather's child is still alive. Which tax issue most directly applies?","choices":["The gift is taxable only if the grandfather's estate exceeds the exemption","The generation-skipping transfer tax, because the gift skips the child's generation","No tax applies, because gifts to grandchildren are always excluded","The gift is taxed only at the grandson's income tax rate"],"correct":1,"rationale":["Wrong — the gift is not tested against the estate tax exemption at death; the GST rules apply at the time of the transfer.","Correct — a gift from a grandparent to a grandchild skips a generation, so the GST tax applies unless the amount is covered by the GST exemption or the annual exclusion.","Wrong — gifts to grandchildren are not always excluded; the annual exclusion and GST exemption must be used to avoid tax.","Wrong — gift tax and GST tax are not measured by the recipient's income tax rate."],"explanation":"Generation-skipping transfer tax applies to transfers to skip persons (grandchildren) beyond the GST exemption and annual exclusion. The GST exemption is separate from the gift exemption and matches the estate exemption amount."},
{"id":"xg-14","domain":"estate","sub":"Sources for estate liquidity","q":"A business owner dies, and most of his estate is an illiquid closely held business. His estate owes estate tax due within nine months. Which planning tool best addresses the liquidity need?","choices":["A will that gives the business to his children","A revocable living trust with no insurance","Life insurance owned by an irrevocable life insurance trust, which provides liquidity to pay the estate tax","A joint checking account with his spouse"],"correct":2,"rationale":["Wrong — passing the business to the children does not generate cash to pay the tax.","Wrong — a revocable trust does not provide cash for estate taxes, and the business remains illiquid.","Correct — insurance owned by an ILIT provides cash outside the estate to pay estate taxes without adding to the taxable estate.","Wrong — a joint account does not solve the liquidity problem for an illiquid business."],"explanation":"Estate liquidity: life insurance held in an ILIT is a common source of cash to pay estate tax without inflating the taxable estate, plus options like installment payments under IRC §6166."},
{"id":"xg-15","domain":"estate","sub":"Types, features, and taxation of trusts","q":"A client names his trust as the beneficiary of his traditional IRA. The trust is drafted without special provisions. Which issue is most important to consider?","choices":["Naming a trust always disqualifies the IRA from the 10-year rule","Naming a trust makes the IRA immune from estate tax","The trust can receive the IRA with no tax consequences, so no further review is needed","The trust may be treated as a non-person beneficiary with a shorter payout period, which can accelerate income tax on the IRA"],"correct":3,"rationale":["Wrong — the 10-year rule can still apply when the trust is properly drafted, so it is not disqualified by default.","Wrong — the IRA is still included in the estate for estate tax purposes, so naming a trust does not avoid estate tax.","Wrong — a trust beneficiary without look-through provisions can face faster distribution rules and accelerated income tax.","Correct — if the trust does not qualify as a see-through trust, it may be subject to faster payout rules, so the trust must be drafted carefully."],"explanation":"See-through (look-through) trust rules determine whether a trust beneficiary can use individual payout periods. Poor drafting can accelerate income tax on inherited IRAs."}
];
QUESTIONS.push(...COVERAGE_EXAM_QUESTIONS);
